1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,040
 in this lecture we're going to learn about Amazon Q Business.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,040
 So what is Amazon Q Business?

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:08,680
 Amazon Q Business is a fully managed,

4
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:11,240
 generative AI powered assistant.

5
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:14,240
 So the core objective of Amazon Q Business

6
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:18,360
 is to improve the productivity of an employee.

7
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:21,920
 So how does Amazon Q improve the productivity of an employee

8
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:24,920
 by helping generate content

9
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:28,800
 which can act as a baseline for the business user.

10
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,240
 Then it can summarize data.

11
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:34,040
 So most employees have to go through

12
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:37,120
 a large number of documents, reports and so on.

13
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,400
 So what it can do is it can summarize the data

14
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,200
 for the employees and improve its productivity.

15
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,480
 A lot of times let's say you are a marketing manager

16
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:49,160
 or a sales manager, you need to go through

17
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:53,080
 a large number of different organization data sources

18
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:55,400
 to come up with maybe a proposal

19
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:58,920
 or a brand campaign and so on.

20
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,800
 So you will have to go through all the different data sources

21
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,720
 may be some data is in the Salesforce,

22
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:06,640
 some is in the database, some is in the Google Drive.

23
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:11,800
 So what Amazon Q allows you is to chat with enterprise data.

24
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:14,920
 So you can have a chat based interface

25
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:18,360
 and then you can post query irrespective of where the data

26
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:21,680
 is whether it's in the Google Drive S3 Salesforce.

27
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:26,280
 It will give you a response that is relevant to you.

28
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:31,640
 Then it also has plugins such as GRA, ServiceNow, et cetera,

29
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:34,600
 where you could give the commands in natural language

30
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,600
 and say create a ticket for the task

31
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,800
 and it will automatically go ahead and create a task

32
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:42,440
 in the GRA or ServiceNow for you.

33
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:45,240
 You can think of Amazon Q as a chat GPT

34
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:47,680
 with enterprise controls.

35
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,000
 Now let's try and understand how Amazon Q for business

36
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,320
 can improve the productivity by taking a look

37
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:57,480
 at a typical day of a marketing manager.

38
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:59,920
 So let's say we have a marketing manager

39
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,840
 that works in a large Fortune 500 company

40
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,000
 and her name is Anisha.

41
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,360
 Now the objective of the organization

42
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:12,360
 is to improve the productivity of Anisha with Amazon Q.

43
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:16,360
 Now let's see what are the four key tasks for Anisha

44
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:18,680
 in a day and how Amazon Q can help.

45
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:23,040
 Now let's say the first one is she got a feedback report

46
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:24,840
 on the impact of marketing campaign

47
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:27,160
 and it's about 20 pages long.

48
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:29,440
 They had launched a campaign few weeks back

49
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:31,640
 and now they have got a feedback report.

50
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:36,320
 Now Amazon Q can help summarize this 20 page report

51
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:38,560
 into maybe a couple of pages

52
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:41,520
 which will help improve the productivity of Anisha

53
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:43,000
 to some extent.

54
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,040
 Now let's say the second task for her today is

55
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:48,600
 she wants to send an email to a prospective client

56
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:52,600
 outlying the steps to launch a campaign for a juice trick

57
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:57,760
 and again Amazon Q can help you by generating

58
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:00,560
 draft content for her.

59
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,400
 Then let's say she wants to understand

60
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:06,520
 what are the brand guidelines within the organization

61
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,360
 because she is new to the organization

62
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:12,880
 and these brand guidelines are spread across

63
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:14,480
 large number of enterprise data sources

64
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:20,280
 maybe some are in SharePoint, some others are in Salesforce

65
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:21,800
 and so on.

66
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:26,520
 So Amazon Q has a chat interface through which you can ask

67
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:30,960
 queries to Amazon Q and it's going to search all the

68
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:35,200
 enterprise data sources and give back a response to you.

69
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:39,480
 And then finally let's say she wants to assign some tasks

70
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:42,680
 to her direct report is Amazon Q provides you

71
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:46,600
 with a plugin through which you can write the task

72
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:49,800
 in natural language in the chat interface

73
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:53,360
 and it's going to create a ticket in the zero.

74
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:56,520
 Okay, now let's take a quick look at Amazon Q

75
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:58,480
 for business architecture.

76
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:01,680
 Okay, now there are two type of users for Amazon Q.

77
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:05,680
 One is the admin user which manages the application

78
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:08,600
 allows you to connect with different data sources

79
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:11,920
 within the enterprise ads users and groups to the application

80
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:14,160
 and deletes and so on.

81
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:16,560
 And then you have the end user.

82
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:19,840
 Okay, now we have the Amazon Q application

83
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:24,000
 and it can connect to large number of different enterprise

84
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,280
 data sources sometimes call us reviewers.

85
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:30,240
 So there are more than 40 pre-built connectors.

86
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:34,160
 So you have the S3, RDS, Google Drive, Salesforce

87
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:37,400
 and many others as we'll see in the demo.

88
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,200
 So first thing you'll have to do while building an app

89
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:44,480
 on Amazon Q is to identify the data sources

90
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:49,480
 and then Amazon Q will ingest the document contents

91
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:54,200
 and permissions on who has access to these documents.

92
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:57,560
 Then Amazon Q also supports large number of plugins

93
00:04:57,560 --> 00:04:59,960
 like Jira and ServiceNow.

94
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:04,320
 You can establish connection with Jira and ServiceNow.

95
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:06,720
 So once you have accomplished this task

96
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:11,720
 of connecting the Amazon Q with the data source and plugins,

97
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:15,600
 the next thing you need to do is you need to connect it

98
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:19,280
 with some form of authentication and authorization

99
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:23,360
 and that is done using the IM Identity Center

100
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:26,680
 where this admin will create the users

101
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:30,920
 and groups that will have access to Amazon Q

102
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:33,720
 from the end user perspective.

103
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:38,240
 Okay, now let's say you have created the users and groups.

104
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:40,840
 Now let's say we have this business user Anisha

105
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:44,800
 who is a marketing manager and she is a query regarding

106
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:48,280
 what are the branding guidelines within the organization.

107
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:53,280
 And let's say they are within this PDF in the S3 bucket.

108
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:56,880
 So once she sends the query to Amazon Q,

109
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:01,480
 Amazon Q will validate her authentication credentials

110
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:05,560
 and group permissions, then it will refer to the document

111
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,600
 on what are the kind of permissions

112
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,800
 that are highlighted in this document

113
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:14,960
 that she's seeking to access where the query.

114
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:17,040
 And if the permissions match,

115
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:19,080
 then she's going to get back a response

116
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:21,520
 and it could be a filter response.

117
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:24,800
 If her permissions that have been defined

118
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:29,320
 in the IM Identity Center does not match to the permission

119
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:32,520
 that are required to access this document.

120
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:35,720
 So hopefully the business architecture for Amazon Q

121
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:38,040
 is clear to you, I'll see you in the next lecture.

122
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:38,880
 Thank you.

